@startuml proto-ds18b20
' ===========================================================================
' proto-ds18b20.puml - (a) the DS18B20 1-Wire protocol engine, both sides.
'
' 1-Wire inverts the DHT11's problem. The DHT11 is sampled BETWEEN two windows;
' a DS18B20 read slot is sampled INSIDE the slot, and it is the DEVICE that
' initiates: after the host's reset the sensor answers with a presence pulse, so
' for the second half of the exchange the firmware is reading a line the sensor
' is driving.
'
' The lesson the whole file is built on is the split between the two kinds of
' wait:
'   where the wire ANNOUNCES an event  -> WAIT for the event (spin on the pin)
'   where the wire does not             -> COUNT
' Steps 1, 3, 4 and the inner delay of step 5 are counted; step 2 and the first
' half of step 5 are edge waits. The first DHT11 design counted its way through
' the sensor's bit periods and drifted 40 us over twenty zero bits.
' ===========================================================================

title DS18B20 - the 1-Wire protocol engine on both sides\n<font size=12>reset, presence, 0xCC, 0xBE, two read bytes; LSB first, sampled INSIDE the slot</font>

hide empty description
hide stereotype
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam state {
  BackgroundColor White
  BorderColor #33555
  FontName monospaced
}
skinparam note {
  BackgroundColor #FFFBE6
  BorderColor #C8B800
  FontName monospaced
  FontSize 11
}

' ---------------------------------------------------------------- EMITTER --
state "FIRMWARE SIDE - firmware/ds18b20.pe" as EMIT {

  [*] --> CLAIM
  CLAIM : PINOE = 0x00, TXPIN = OW_DATA, PINOE = OW_DATA\n<b>1-Wire idles HIGH, and the idle state is the pull-up</b>

  state "RESET\nthe host's pulse" as RST
  RST : TXPIN = 0x00 - the line goes LOW\ndmem[12] = 0 (the delay's return phase)\nCOARSE pair (4,40), OW_RST = 58: 485.5 us\n<b>A COUNTED DELAY, because nothing on the wire\nsays when 480 us is up</b>

  state "PH0\nrelease, then settle" as PH0
  PH0 : PINOE = 0x00 - RELEASE, the sensor may pull low\ndmem[12] = 1\nFINE pair (2,13) from here on: one outer step\nis 69 clocks = 1.15 us, which is what a 5 us\nslot needs\nOW_T15 = 14, a 15 us settle

  state "PH1\nwait for the PRESENCE" as PH1
  PH1 : spin until LOW, then spin until HIGH.\n<b>BOTH edges are waited for, with no counted 15 us\nbetween them</b> - the pulse's length is the\nsensor's to choose (60-240 us) and the host\ncannot know it. If no sensor is there the first\nloop never exits, which is correct for a bus\nwith no timeout in this ISA.

  state "WRITE A BIT\nLSB first, no shifting" as WB
  WB : the bit to send is the byte's own BIT 0, so\nthe loop needs no shift: read the byte, mask\nbit 0, send, SHR the byte so the next bit 0 is\nthe next bit of the frame.\ndmem[6] holds the byte to send, dmem[2] the\nbits left. EIGHT slots per byte.\n\n<b>THE WRITE PATH MUST CLAIM THE PIN.</b>\n1-Wire writes DRIVE: release-then-set-the-level\ndoes not pull the line down, and the pin is\nRELEASED at ph0, so TXPIN = 0 alone would leave\nthe line HIGH and the sensor would see a 1 for\nevery bit. The model caught this - it decoded 0\nwrite bits because the line never went low.

  state "a 1:\nshort low, then release" as W1
  W1 : PINOE = OW_DATA, TXPIN = 0x00\nOW_T5 = 5: 5 us of low, the datasheet's 1-15 us\nthen PH2, which RELEASES and waits\nOW_T55 = 49: the rest of the slot, ~55 us

  state "a 0:\nhold low for the WHOLE slot" as W0
  W0 : PINOE = OW_DATA, TXPIN = 0x00, and it\nSTAYS: the phase goes to 3, not to 2\nOW_T65 = 57: 65 us, the datasheet's ~60 us\nthen PH3 releases and the next slot starts high

  state "PH5\nthe byte is sent" as PH5
  PH5 : SHR the byte, dmem[2]--, more bits?\ndmem[7]-- : 2 then 1 then 0. After the decrement,\n0 means BOTH 0xCC and 0xBE are out.\n<b>THE DELAY AND THE PROTOCOL MUST HAVE DISJOINT\ndmem OWNERSHIP.</b> This counter was on dmem[10],\nwhich is the DELAY ROUTINE's middle-counter\nTARGET - so every delay overwrote it and the\nbyte transition read whatever the delay left\nbehind. A shared slot between them is not a\nwarning, it is a lost phase transition.

  state "READ A BIT\nthe hybrid wait" as RB
  RB : the host INITIATES: PINOE = OW_DATA,\nTXPIN = 0x00 - a 6 us initiation pulse, because\nthe SENSOR presents the bit as a LEVEL held\nacross a known instant.\nOW_T40 = 23: a fixed 25.4 us wait, then\n\n<b>A previous version SPUN for the sensor's own\nfalling edge here, and it raced the model's\ntransitions: the line was still low from the\ninitiation, so the loop exited at once and every\nsample landed a whole slot early. Counting to a\nfixed instant cannot race.</b>

  state "PH4\nSAMPLE THE LEVEL" as PH4
  PH4 : IN A, PIN; AND A, OW_DATA\nJNZ rb_was_zero\n\n<b>RELEASED (high) is a ZERO on the wire;\nPULLED DOWN (low) is a ONE - the OPPOSITE of a\nwrite slot, where the host pulling the line down\nIS the zero.</b> The first version read the\nopposite and both bytes came back EXACTLY\ncomplemented: 0x2b as 0xd4 and 0x01 as 0xfe.\n\nthen SHR the accumulator with the new bit at the\nTOP, so the FIRST bit read slides down to bit0 by\nthe eighth: 1-Wire is LSB first.

  state "DLY1/DLY2/DLY3\nthe delay" as DLY
  DLY : total(n1,n2,n3) = (n1-1)*(10 + (n2-1)*(4*n3+7)) + 4 clocks\nTWO pairs:\n  (4,40)  511 clocks = 8.5 us a step - the reset\n  (2,13)   69 clocks = 1.15 us a step - the slots\n\n<b>A DISPATCH IS A FUNCTION OVER EVERY PHASE THE\nPROGRAM USES.</b> dmem[12] carries the phase and a\nsubtract chain picks the return address, because\nthe ISA cannot return to seven addresses. Two\nbugs lived in this chain, both from patching one\ndispatch for two different loops: it first handled\n0-3 and sent everything >= 4 to ph4, which\ndropped a write-1's high period into the read path;\nand when the read loop was given phases 4 and 5\nit collided with the write loop's 4 and 5.

  state "PARK\ndone" as PARK
  PARK : dmem[14] = 1 - both bytes are in. THE TB'S\nOBSERVABLE, so "hung mid-byte" (0) and\n"finished" (1) are different failures.\nTXPIN = 0x00, then JMP halt: no HALT in the ISA.

  CLAIM --> RST
  RST --> DLY
  DLY --> PH0 : dmem[12] == 0
  PH0 --> DLY
  DLY --> PH1 : dmem[12] == 1
  PH1 --> WB : presence found
  WB --> W1 : bit 0 set
  WB --> W0 : bit 0 clear
  W1 --> DLY
  DLY --> PH2 : dmem[12] == 2, a 1's low is over
  PH2 --> DLY
  DLY --> PH5 : dmem[12] == 5, the 55 us are over
  PH5 --> WB : more bits, or the next byte
  W0 --> DLY
  DLY --> PH3 : dmem[12] == 3, a 0's slot is over
  PH3 --> PH5 : the byte is sent
  PH5 --> RB : dmem[7] hit 0: BOTH commands are out
  RB --> DLY
  DLY --> PH4 : dmem[12] == 4, the sample instant
  PH4 --> RB : dmem[3] != 0, or the next byte
  PH4 --> PARK : dmem[8] hit 0
}

' --------------------------------------------------------------- SENSOR/SRC --
state "SENSOR SIDE - tb/tb_pe_soc_ds18b20.v" as SRC {

  [*] --> WATCH
  WATCH : the model is written FROM THE DATASHEET,\nnot from the firmware - which is why it was\nright when the firmware was wrong\n\n<b>it drives tRDV at the datasheet's MAXIMUM\n(15 us), so "sampled too early" is falsifiable</b>\nand its read edges come off the PADS rather than\noff its own state, which is what stopped it\nlosing the master's next release while still\ninside the previous bit's hold

  state "RESET + PRESENCE" as RP
  RP : host low >= 480 us, then the sensor\npulls low for 60-240 us and releases

  state "COMMANDS" as CMD
  CMD : 0xCC SKIP ROM, then 0xBE READ SCRATCHPAD,\nLSB first, eight slots each, and both are\nDECODED FROM THE WIRE

  state "SCRATCHPAD" as SP
  SP : sixteen read slots, the model's own\ndecode, and the firmware's two banked bytes\n<b>2b 01</b>

  state "CHECK\nwhat is claimed" as CHK
  CHK : 1. the reset pulse is > 480 us\n2. the presence pulse is inside 60..240 us\n   AND the host's pin_oe is released on every\n   clock of it\n3. every WRITE slot's low period is inside its\n   own band (1-15 us for a 1, 60-120 for a 0)\n4. exactly 16 read slots\n5. every read slot's initiation pulse is inside\n   1-15 us\n6. the SAMPLE INSTANT is inside the sensor's\n   data window with >= 2 us of margin at BOTH\n   ends\n7. dmem[0..1] == 2b 01, LSB first\n8. dmem[14] == 1

  WATCH --> RP
  RP --> CMD
  CMD --> SP
  SP --> CHK
  CHK --> WATCH
}

EMIT -[#3A7,hidden]-> SRC : ONE wire, open-drain in law\nand in the model, and the direction of\nthe pull-down REVERSES between the two halves

note bottom of EMIT
  <b>Why the polarity is different in the two directions,
  and why the two cannot share one sample idiom.</b>

  In a 1-Wire READ slot a ONE is the line LOW: the
  slave holds it down for the rest of the slot. In a
  WRITE slot it is the other way about, because there
  the host is the one pulling down. The read path
  first borrowed the write path's sample test, and
  both bytes came back exactly complemented.

  A sensor that transmits its data inverted still
  transmits DATA. Both bytes still had eight bits and
  a plausible number of ones. Nothing in the run looks
  wrong until the bytes are compared - which is why the
  way to tell a polarity bug from a protocol bug is to
  look at the ACCUMULATOR, not the waveform.
end note

note bottom of RB
  <b>Why the read is "wait, then count to a fixed instant"
  and not "wait for an edge".</b>

  The obvious version spins for the sensor's own falling
  edge. It races: the line is still LOW from the host's
  own initiation pulse, so the loop exits at once and
  every sample lands a whole slot early. The version
  that works is release, wait 25.4 us, SAMPLE THE LEVEL -
  which is the DHT11 lesson applied the other way round.
  There, the host had to synchronise because the sensor's
  bit PERIOD varies. Here the host has to count because
  the sensor's bit VALUE is a LEVEL, and a level read at a
  fixed instant needs no edge at all.
end note

note bottom of SRC
  <b>Why the open-drain bit is deliberately NOT set.</b>

  pe_pinmux has an OD bit and this is the open-drain
  case, so leaving it clear looks like an omission. It
  is a decision, and it costs something: with OD set, a
  pin holding 1 RELEASES rather than drives high, so
  with OD set a pin holding 1 READS BACK AS RELEASED -
  a write of 1 and a write of 0 look identical on the
  pin, and the edge-wait loops cannot see the sensor's
  pulses at all.

  OD buys nothing here and costs the one thing the pin
  read-back is for. The i2c_pins.pe idiom - "write out=1
  or out=0 and never touch oe or od again" - is right
  for a bus where the LEVEL IS THE DATA. 1-Wire is the
  opposite case: the data is a TIME and the level is
  just a carrier for it.
end note

@enduml
